 5. 🏛️ Zelensky turns to Capitol Hill 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 Photo illustration: Sarah Grillo/Axios. Photos: Al Drago/Bloomberg, Mert Gokhankoc/ dia image via Getty Images

 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Ukrainian President Volodymyr Zelensky lobbied a bipartisan group of senators yesterday to press the Trump administration to remove what he claimed were restrictions on intelligence sharing and weapons supplies, Axios' Barak Ravid writes. 

 Why it matters: Ukrainian officials say the Trump administration has quietly imposed these limits without admitting it publicly in an attempt to press Zelensky to accept a deal to end the war that includes more concessions to Russia. Behind the scenes: During a virtual meeting with a bipartisan group of senators led by Thom Tillis (R-N.C.) and Richard Blumenthal (D-Conn.), Zelensky laid out the various problems Ukraine has been experiencing since the Miami meeting.

 Elon Musk's Starlink and the U.S. military's Starshield satellite systems remain partially restricted — despite claims by the White House to the contrary. On three occasions in the past week, Ukraine didn't receive early warnings from the U.S. about Russian ballistic missile attacks, Zelensky said, suggesting it was a sign that intelligence sharing has been curbed. Share this story . 

 
 
